Goal:
To login as bandit14 using the private that is given in bandit13.

Connect:
ssh -i priv.key bandit14@bandit.labs.overthewire.org -p 2220

Approach:
First would be to copy that private key to our local system as using that private key to login 
as bandit14 from localhost is denied.
chmod 700 must be done because ssh expects any private key file to have very stringent permissions.

Command:
scp -p 2220 bandit13@bandit.labs.overthewire.org:/home/bandit13/sshkey.private .
chmod 700 sshkey.private
ssh -i sshkey.private bandit14@bandit.labs.overthewire.org -p 2220
cat /etc/bandit_pass/bandit14

Breakdown:
Copy key: scp copies the private key from bandit13 to your local machine.
Permissions: chmod 700 secures the private key so SSH accepts it.
Login: ssh -i sshkey.private uses the private key to authenticate as bandit14.
Password: cat /etc/bandit_pass/bandit14 displays Bandit 14's password.

Result:
Successfully copied the private key and used it to log in as bandit14.
Retrieved the Bandit 14 password from /etc/bandit_pass/bandit14.

Key Takeaway:
Private SSH keys enable passwordless authentication when correctly configured.
Correct file permissions are essential for SSH to accept a private key.
